  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 323 AM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nStrong upper ridging continues to build across the central Plains \nWednesday, with a closed low off the coast of California. While \nstrong ridging will remain through Thursday, the closed low will \ngradually move onshore through the day, and into the Rocky Mountain \nregion by Friday morning. This pattern will generally lead to \nwarmer, drier conditions through the mid week. Daytime highs are \nexpected to be in the low to mid 70s across western and north \ncentral Nebraska Wednesday and Thursday, with southeasterly winds \ngusting up to 20 - 30 mph Thursday afternoon. By Friday, the ridge \nbegins to get squished with the upper trough crossing the Rockies. \nThis will lead to an increase in anomalous temperatures for the area \nwith H850 temps being 20 to 23C across western to southwest \nNebraska. This translates to daytime highs ranging in the mid to \nupper 70s across region, with southwest Nebraska having a low-end \n(10 - 20 percent) but not impossible chance of reaching 80. This \nwould put highs 10 to 15 degrees above climatological norms for the \nseason. Winds will be the strongest Friday afternoon through the \nevening hours with sustained southerly winds ranging from 20 to 25 \nmph, with strongest gusts being east of a line from Ogallala to \nValentine. These gusts are expected to be in the 30 - 40 mph range \nbut there is a 30 to 50 percent chance of gusts being in excess of \n40 mph. Luckily, marginal humidities (35 - 50 percent) will \noverlap with these strong winds, so fire concerns should be \nlimited. \n\nIn addition, a shortwave ahead of the upper low will move into the \nNorthern Plains Friday afternoon. This will drive a cold front \nsoutheastward across the region. This could provide enough lift to a \nstream of moisture making its way up from the Gulf, bringing \nincreased chances for showers as well as a few thunderstorms Friday \nevening through Saturday. Even with increased precipitation chances, \naccumulations are still likely to remain fairly modest with north \ncentral Nebraska having a 20 - 40 percent chance of seeing greater \nthan a tenth of an inch during this time frame. \n\nThe upper low lifts into the Dakotas, while the cold front pushes \nout of our area. In the wake of the front, daytime highs over the \nweekend and early next week are expected to be in the low to mid \n60s, which would be on par to slightly warmer than seasonal\nnorms.",
